1
00:00:01,914 --> 00:00:04,482
[instrumental music]

2
00:00:31,074 --> 00:00:33,468
[music continues]

3
00:00:33,555 --> 00:00:34,512
[thud]

4
00:00:39,517 --> 00:00:41,780
[sizzling]

5
00:00:48,091 --> 00:00:49,049
Hello?

6
00:00:50,572 --> 00:00:51,616
[man laughing]

7
00:00:51,703 --> 00:00:54,097
[sizzling continues]

8
00:00:54,184 --> 00:00:55,577
'What the hell?'

9
00:00:55,664 --> 00:00:58,536
[sizzling continues]

10
00:01:00,321 --> 00:01:02,323
[intense music]

11
00:01:07,328 --> 00:01:10,026
[male #1]
'I appreciate your people coming
in on this, Agent Lisbon.'

12
00:01:10,113 --> 00:01:12,463
'Haven't handled many
homicides. None, in fact.'

13
00:01:14,161 --> 00:01:15,814
Frankly, I'm looking
to learn from you.

14
00:01:15,901 --> 00:01:17,381
Not a problem, Deputy Fisher.

15
00:01:17,468 --> 00:01:19,209
Small town.
We don't get much trouble.

16
00:01:19,296 --> 00:01:21,951
- Well, that's a shame.
- It is?

17
00:01:22,038 --> 00:01:23,648
Trouble's your business.

18
00:01:23,735 --> 00:01:25,041
You could say.

19
00:01:26,695 --> 00:01:29,306
'Victim's Alan Foster,
43 years old'

20
00:01:29,393 --> 00:01:31,439
married, no children. Architect.

21
00:01:31,526 --> 00:01:33,005
He bought the house
a few months back

22
00:01:33,093 --> 00:01:35,399
said he was gonna rehab it.

23
00:01:35,486 --> 00:01:36,748
Was the wife
with him last night?

24
00:01:36,835 --> 00:01:38,098
Uh, no, she was in the city.

25
00:01:38,185 --> 00:01:39,490
They have a house
in San Francisco.

26
00:01:39,577 --> 00:01:41,362
I sent a man
to break the news.

27
00:01:41,449 --> 00:01:43,233
Was anybody else
in the house with him?

28
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:45,801
'Nobody as far as we can tell.
I don't know what to say.'

29
00:01:45,888 --> 00:01:47,803
'The house was locked up tighter
than a drum when I got here.'

30
00:01:47,890 --> 00:01:50,066
Took me half an hour
just to shut off the alarm.

31
00:01:50,153 --> 00:01:51,546
Cho, check
the alarm system.

32
00:01:51,633 --> 00:01:52,764
Either the victim
let somebody in

33
00:01:52,851 --> 00:01:54,636
or they broke
in someplace.

34
00:01:54,723 --> 00:01:57,552
It's a shame.
Foster seemed like a nice guy.

35
00:01:57,639 --> 00:02:00,946
Whoa, that's a lie.
You didn't like him one bit.

36
00:02:01,033 --> 00:02:03,340
- I-I didn't say that.
- Yes, you did.

37
00:02:03,427 --> 00:02:05,429
You thought he was rich,
arrogant, pretentious

38
00:02:05,516 --> 00:02:09,172
an interloper. He wore those
trendy architect's glasses.

39
00:02:09,259 --> 00:02:12,306
[chuckles]
Did you kill him?

40
00:02:12,393 --> 00:02:13,350
- Did I..
- 'Hmm?'

41
00:02:13,437 --> 00:02:16,136
- Hmm?
- 'No.'

42
00:02:18,181 --> 00:02:19,400
No.

43
00:02:19,487 --> 00:02:22,359
[instrumental music]

44
00:02:22,446 --> 00:02:24,187
What kind of question is that?

45
00:02:27,843 --> 00:02:29,018
[sighs]

46
00:02:32,195 --> 00:02:34,589
He's got something in his hand.

47
00:02:34,676 --> 00:02:36,721
- Jane, gloves.
- Right.

48
00:02:45,382 --> 00:02:47,297
[Lisbon]
'A brass button
with the letter B?'

49
00:02:49,038 --> 00:02:52,302
That mean something
to you, deputy?

50
00:02:52,389 --> 00:02:53,303
No.

51
00:02:56,001 --> 00:02:57,177
What's this guy's problem?

52
00:02:57,264 --> 00:02:59,179
That would take way
too long to answer.

53
00:03:01,572 --> 00:03:02,878
Let's take a look inside.

54
00:03:02,965 --> 00:03:04,401
Okay. It's this way.

55
00:03:05,359 --> 00:03:08,188
[music continues]

56
00:03:17,327 --> 00:03:20,243
Clear signs of struggle
rules out suicide.

57
00:03:20,330 --> 00:03:23,203
That, and suicides usually open
the window before they jump.

58
00:03:26,815 --> 00:03:28,556
What's troubling you, Fisher?

59
00:03:28,643 --> 00:03:29,818
What? Nothing.
What are you talking about?

60
00:03:29,905 --> 00:03:31,385
That button made
your pupils dilate.

61
00:03:31,472 --> 00:03:33,517
Now you've gone pale.

62
00:03:33,604 --> 00:03:35,476
What's up, Fisher?
What's troubling you?

63
00:03:37,565 --> 00:03:40,829
Well, it occurred
to me that the button

64
00:03:40,916 --> 00:03:42,352
could stand for Beckworth

65
00:03:42,439 --> 00:03:44,528
'and I got a little
chill, that's all.'

66
00:03:44,615 --> 00:03:47,183
- Who's Beckworth?
- Walter Beckworth.

67
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:50,230
He was the original owner
of this house, built it.

68
00:03:50,317 --> 00:03:52,841
Died 80 something years ago.

69
00:03:52,928 --> 00:03:56,061
People say he...
haunts the place.

70
00:03:56,148 --> 00:03:57,280
I've heard the stories
since I was a kid

71
00:03:57,367 --> 00:03:59,630
but nobody believes them.

72
00:03:59,717 --> 00:04:01,153
Right.

73
00:04:01,241 --> 00:04:02,981
Guy from the security
company ran the alarm history.

74
00:04:03,068 --> 00:04:04,374
It's been armed
from six last night

75
00:04:04,461 --> 00:04:06,115
until the deputies
turned it off this morning.

76
00:04:06,202 --> 00:04:07,943
No alarms
or interruptions of service.

77
00:04:08,030 --> 00:04:09,510
As far as I can tell

78
00:04:09,597 --> 00:04:10,815
no one's entered
or left this building

79
00:04:10,902 --> 00:04:12,077
except the victim.

80
00:04:12,164 --> 00:04:13,470
But that's impossible.

81
00:04:15,429 --> 00:04:16,560
Isn't it?

82
00:04:18,475 --> 00:04:19,389
Spooky.

83
00:04:20,956 --> 00:04:23,741
[theme music]

84
00:04:32,402 --> 00:04:33,751
I'm not saying volunteering
is better work than this

85
00:04:33,838 --> 00:04:35,362
but it makes me feel better
at the end of the day.

86
00:04:35,449 --> 00:04:38,365
I see people smiling
because of what I've done.

87
00:04:38,452 --> 00:04:41,237
There's something,
I don't know, joyous in it.

88
00:04:41,324 --> 00:04:43,283
How often do we see
people smile in this job?

89
00:04:43,370 --> 00:04:45,981
Leaving out crazy? Never.
The coroner took Foster's body.

90
00:04:46,068 --> 00:04:47,809
I'm gonna get his wife
and bring her to the office.

91
00:04:47,896 --> 00:04:48,984
Lisbon and Jane
are up on the porch.

92
00:04:49,071 --> 00:04:50,638
Okay, thanks.

93
00:04:50,725 --> 00:04:52,292
She's volunteering
at a homeless kitchen.

94
00:04:53,945 --> 00:04:55,164
- That's cool.
- There's a man there.

95
00:04:55,251 --> 00:04:57,209
I think she's, uh, you know.

96
00:04:57,297 --> 00:04:58,559
Banging a homeless guy?

97
00:04:58,646 --> 00:05:01,562
No! A volunteer.
A chef. And she's not..

98
00:05:01,649 --> 00:05:05,174
I don't know.
Not yet...maybe. I think..

99
00:05:05,261 --> 00:05:07,219
I don't know...no.

100
00:05:09,570 --> 00:05:10,962
Dude, you need counseling.

101
00:05:12,050 --> 00:05:14,270
[instrumental music]

102
00:05:18,927 --> 00:05:20,711
[Lisbon]
'Somehow the killer
got into the house'

103
00:05:20,798 --> 00:05:22,887
without tripping the alarm
or turning it off.

104
00:05:22,974 --> 00:05:24,889
So the killer didn't come
through the front door.

105
00:05:24,976 --> 00:05:27,065
[Lisbon]
'Probably found some kind
of hole in the system.'

106
00:05:27,152 --> 00:05:28,632
Check the grounds
and inside the house.

107
00:05:28,719 --> 00:05:31,635
- Find out how they got in.
- On it.

108
00:05:31,722 --> 00:05:33,202
Grace, you're
the spiritual sort.

109
00:05:33,289 --> 00:05:35,465
Do you believe in ghosts?

110
00:05:35,552 --> 00:05:37,206
I believe the spirit
survives death

111
00:05:37,293 --> 00:05:39,513
and a troubled spirit
can make itself known.

112
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:41,689
Oh, come on. You don't seriously
think there's a ghost here.

113
00:05:41,776 --> 00:05:43,647
Meh. I don't know.

114
00:05:45,257 --> 00:05:46,302
If you see one, let me know.

115
00:05:46,389 --> 00:05:49,131
[instrumental music]

116
00:05:53,962 --> 00:05:55,703
How long had your husband been
staying at Beckworth Mansion

117
00:05:55,790 --> 00:05:57,618
Mrs. Foster?

118
00:05:57,705 --> 00:05:59,097
Off and on for about three weeks

119
00:05:59,184 --> 00:06:00,272
since we closed on it.

120
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:02,013
Why? It's pretty run-down.

121
00:06:02,100 --> 00:06:04,538
To get a feel for the house
before he worked on it.

122
00:06:04,625 --> 00:06:06,757
He said you should
spend a month in a house

123
00:06:06,844 --> 00:06:09,064
before you thought
about changing it.

124
00:06:09,151 --> 00:06:11,675
He's conscientious that way.

125
00:06:11,762 --> 00:06:14,243
Or was conscientious that way.

126
00:06:17,638 --> 00:06:19,117
We can take a break
if you'd like.

127
00:06:19,204 --> 00:06:21,250
No, no, no. Let's,
let's just get through it.

128
00:06:21,337 --> 00:06:23,992
So you were at home the night
of your husband's murder?

129
00:06:24,079 --> 00:06:25,167
Yes.

130
00:06:25,254 --> 00:06:26,560
We have to ask

131
00:06:26,647 --> 00:06:28,170
is there anyone
who can verify that?

132
00:06:28,257 --> 00:06:30,651
Um, no, I was alone.

133
00:06:30,738 --> 00:06:33,131
Alan had been at
the mansion all week.

134
00:06:33,218 --> 00:06:36,047
Was it common for you and your
husband to be apart that long?

135
00:06:36,134 --> 00:06:39,137
It was not uncommon
when he started a project.

136
00:06:39,224 --> 00:06:41,488
He...got immersed.

137
00:06:41,575 --> 00:06:43,533
Who might have wanted
to hurt your husband?

138
00:06:43,620 --> 00:06:46,362
Oh, I can tell you
who was angry with him.

139
00:06:46,449 --> 00:06:48,582
- Victoria Abner.
- Who's she?

140
00:06:48,669 --> 00:06:51,062
She's this hippie-dippie
woman who lives in town

141
00:06:51,149 --> 00:06:54,370
and she weaves linens and rugs.

142
00:06:54,457 --> 00:06:56,633
She used to own Beckworth.

143
00:06:56,720 --> 00:06:59,462
She's a descendant
of the man who built it.

144
00:06:59,549 --> 00:07:01,769
And she resented your
husband for buying the place?

145
00:07:01,856 --> 00:07:03,510
But not at first.

146
00:07:03,597 --> 00:07:05,555
There were tax issues
that came with the property

147
00:07:05,642 --> 00:07:07,383
so we thought she'd
be glad to be rid of it.

148
00:07:07,470 --> 00:07:09,820
But...about a week
after we closed

149
00:07:09,907 --> 00:07:13,433
she just blew up, claimed
that Alan stole the house.

150
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:15,391
It was vicious.

151
00:07:15,478 --> 00:07:17,175
Did she ever
threaten your husband?

152
00:07:17,262 --> 00:07:19,743
[Mrs. Foster]
'She sent some
pretty scary e-mails.'

153
00:07:19,830 --> 00:07:20,875
[knocking on glass]

154
00:07:23,660 --> 00:07:24,574
Excuse me.

155
00:07:30,319 --> 00:07:31,276
What?

156
00:07:31,363 --> 00:07:32,364
Walter Beckworth
was killed

157
00:07:32,452 --> 00:07:33,670
in 1928.

158
00:07:33,757 --> 00:07:35,106
Fight over a woman.
You know how?

159
00:07:35,193 --> 00:07:36,499
No, and I don't care.

160
00:07:38,109 --> 00:07:39,807
He was thrown out of
the exact same window

161
00:07:39,894 --> 00:07:42,984
that Alan Foster was
thrown out of. Coincidence?

162
00:07:43,071 --> 00:07:44,942
- Yes.
- Yeah. Well, it's all here.

163
00:07:45,029 --> 00:07:47,641
- Is that, uh, Foster's widow?
- Mm-hmm.

164
00:07:47,728 --> 00:07:49,251
I need to talk to her.

165
00:07:49,338 --> 00:07:51,819
Mrs. Foster, Patrick Jane.

166
00:07:51,906 --> 00:07:55,518
Did your husband see or hear
anything odd at the mansion

167
00:07:55,605 --> 00:07:58,739
strange sightings,
unusual noises, apparitions?

168
00:07:58,826 --> 00:08:02,264
As a matter of fact, Alan did
mention some strange things.

169
00:08:02,351 --> 00:08:03,308
What kind of things?

170
00:08:03,395 --> 00:08:05,267
Um, in the last week or so

171
00:08:05,354 --> 00:08:08,270
he heard some noises
at night, like groans.

172
00:08:08,357 --> 00:08:11,534
He said sometimes it looked
like things were being moved.

173
00:08:11,621 --> 00:08:13,841
I know it's a little creepy,
but Alan wasn't too bothered.

174
00:08:13,928 --> 00:08:15,233
'He was used to old houses.'

175
00:08:16,713 --> 00:08:18,585
What does this have
to do with how he died?

176
00:08:19,803 --> 00:08:21,152
Victoria Abner
has no arrests

177
00:08:21,239 --> 00:08:22,414
but three years ago
a neighbor took out

178
00:08:22,502 --> 00:08:23,981
a restraining order
against her.

179
00:08:24,068 --> 00:08:26,114
Some argument about
the neighbor's dog.

180
00:08:26,201 --> 00:08:28,159
Victoria got a little
in-your-face about it.

181
00:08:28,246 --> 00:08:29,900
Uh, go and see how
mad she really was

182
00:08:29,987 --> 00:08:30,988
about Foster
buying the mansion.

183
00:08:31,075 --> 00:08:32,294
Okay.

184
00:08:32,381 --> 00:08:34,078
And be sure to ask
about the ghost too.

185
00:08:34,165 --> 00:08:36,167
Do notask about ghosts.

186
00:08:38,256 --> 00:08:41,216
Don't you think I know what
you're doing? No ghosts.

187
00:08:41,303 --> 00:08:43,784
The ghost has something
to do with Foster's death.

188
00:08:43,871 --> 00:08:46,351
No, it doesn't,
because ghosts don't exist.

189
00:08:46,438 --> 00:08:49,616
They don't exist in yourmind,
but if you believe they exist

190
00:08:49,703 --> 00:08:51,922
then...they exist.

191
00:08:55,709 --> 00:08:57,145
[Rigsby]
'Anything?'

192
00:08:57,232 --> 00:08:59,582
No pry marks on any
of the frames so far

193
00:08:59,669 --> 00:09:01,715
no footprints
under the windows.

194
00:09:01,802 --> 00:09:04,108
I don't see any
sign of forced entry.

195
00:09:04,195 --> 00:09:05,675
Maybe itwasa ghost.

196
00:09:05,762 --> 00:09:08,025
Ghosts don't throw
200-pound men out windows.

197
00:09:08,112 --> 00:09:09,374
What do ghosts do?

198
00:09:09,461 --> 00:09:11,246
I have no idea.

199
00:09:11,333 --> 00:09:13,335
Let's just check
the other side.

200
00:09:20,342 --> 00:09:22,605
So you gonna keep volunteering
at the, uh, kitchen?

201
00:09:22,692 --> 00:09:24,781
Oh, yeah.

202
00:09:24,868 --> 00:09:27,262
Making a lot of, uh,
new friends, I expect.

203
00:09:27,349 --> 00:09:28,959
I am, yeah, that's
the great thing

204
00:09:29,046 --> 00:09:30,961
about doing volunteer work.

205
00:09:31,048 --> 00:09:32,920
You meet so many new people
that have the same kind

206
00:09:33,007 --> 00:09:34,835
of values
and interests as you.

207
00:09:34,922 --> 00:09:36,314
Right.

208
00:09:36,401 --> 00:09:38,969
- Hey, look at that.
- What?

209
00:09:39,056 --> 00:09:41,276
The lock on the door, looks new.

210
00:09:41,363 --> 00:09:43,147
Someone cut it open.

211
00:09:43,234 --> 00:09:46,150
[door creaking]

212
00:09:46,237 --> 00:09:49,023
[intense music]

213
00:09:49,110 --> 00:09:51,329
Well, if they haven't started
work on the house yet..

214
00:09:59,990 --> 00:10:01,949
All the power tools are missing.

215
00:10:05,039 --> 00:10:05,953
What's there?

216
00:10:07,476 --> 00:10:09,347
You think ghosts smoke filtered?

217
00:10:09,434 --> 00:10:11,785
Boss, someone broke
into a storage area

218
00:10:11,872 --> 00:10:14,048
'stole a whole bunch
of equipment and supplies.'

219
00:10:14,135 --> 00:10:16,050
'Maybe they broke
into the house too.'

220
00:10:19,793 --> 00:10:22,665
[instrumental music]

221
00:10:33,807 --> 00:10:36,418
Can I help you?

222
00:10:36,505 --> 00:10:39,856
I see you specialize
in local history, Mr..

223
00:10:39,943 --> 00:10:41,336
Raimey. Philip Raimey.

224
00:10:41,423 --> 00:10:42,903
Yes, and you are?

225
00:10:42,990 --> 00:10:45,122
Looking for information
on Walter Beckworth.

226
00:10:46,646 --> 00:10:48,473
Are you with the police?

227
00:10:48,560 --> 00:10:49,997
Here about the Foster incident?

228
00:10:50,084 --> 00:10:51,259
Yes.

229
00:10:51,346 --> 00:10:52,739
But don't tell anyone.

230
00:10:52,826 --> 00:10:54,218
Patrick Jane.

231
00:10:54,305 --> 00:10:55,785
Well, Mr. Jane

232
00:10:55,872 --> 00:10:57,787
if it's Beckworth
you're interested in

233
00:10:57,874 --> 00:10:59,746
you've come to
the right place.

234
00:11:00,747 --> 00:11:03,184
[music continues]

235
00:11:03,271 --> 00:11:04,228
Thank you.

236
00:11:07,101 --> 00:11:10,060
Agent Cho, of course
I was angry with Foster.

237
00:11:10,147 --> 00:11:12,846
If someone stole your home,
wouldn't you be angry as well?

238
00:11:12,933 --> 00:11:14,761
- He bought the house, Mrs.--
- You don't understand.

239
00:11:14,848 --> 00:11:16,327
A house you haven't lived
in since you were a child.

240
00:11:16,414 --> 00:11:18,590
That's not the point.

241
00:11:18,678 --> 00:11:20,418
Sorry. I didn't know
you were with somebody.

242
00:11:20,505 --> 00:11:23,639
Oh, this is my nephew, Drew.

243
00:11:23,726 --> 00:11:27,512
Agent Cho is asking
questions about Alan Foster.

244
00:11:27,599 --> 00:11:29,645
He wants to know
why I was so upset

245
00:11:29,732 --> 00:11:31,734
with the man who
stole Beckworth.

246
00:11:31,821 --> 00:11:34,781
- It is a complicated issue.
- No, it's not that complicated.

247
00:11:34,868 --> 00:11:36,391
There are many factors
to be considered

248
00:11:36,478 --> 00:11:39,176
economic and emotional.

249
00:11:39,263 --> 00:11:43,528
My nephew is a lawyer.
He's very sensible.

250
00:11:43,615 --> 00:11:44,965
That's good,
because I'm not.

251
00:11:45,052 --> 00:11:47,097
There are no
factors to consider.

252
00:11:47,184 --> 00:11:49,186
Foster was a thief and a liar.

253
00:11:49,273 --> 00:11:51,536
You couldn't afford
the taxes on the house.

254
00:11:51,623 --> 00:11:52,886
You were grateful when
he first purchased it.

255
00:11:52,973 --> 00:11:55,410
What changed your mind?

256
00:11:55,497 --> 00:11:56,933
You have a very good aura.

257
00:11:57,020 --> 00:11:59,414
Thank you.

258
00:11:59,501 --> 00:12:02,112
Excellent color, deep red..

259
00:12:02,199 --> 00:12:04,245
...grounded, realistic.

260
00:12:05,333 --> 00:12:08,902
And Foster's aura...was pink.

261
00:12:08,989 --> 00:12:10,381
Dirty pink.

262
00:12:10,468 --> 00:12:12,949
That's dishonesty,
fecklessness.

263
00:12:13,036 --> 00:12:16,779
When he came to me, he said
he wanted to preserve Beckworth.

264
00:12:16,866 --> 00:12:18,694
He lied to me.

265
00:12:18,781 --> 00:12:21,305
He wanted to gut it,
my childhood home.

266
00:12:21,392 --> 00:12:22,611
'I couldn't let him do that.'

267
00:12:22,698 --> 00:12:24,656
Look, agent,
the Beckworth mansion

268
00:12:24,744 --> 00:12:27,485
is understandably a very
emotional subject for my family

269
00:12:27,572 --> 00:12:30,401
but whatever she
may have felt or said

270
00:12:30,488 --> 00:12:33,230
my aunt is incapable
of hurting anybody.

271
00:12:33,317 --> 00:12:35,276
Where were you last night?

272
00:12:36,059 --> 00:12:37,669
In my home.

273
00:12:37,757 --> 00:12:39,367
In my bed.

274
00:12:39,454 --> 00:12:40,977
Not killing Alan Foster.

275
00:12:41,064 --> 00:12:42,762
- What about you?
- I was here.

276
00:12:42,849 --> 00:12:44,111
I've been staying
with my aunt on and off

277
00:12:44,198 --> 00:12:46,156
ever since she was
forced to sell the place.

278
00:12:46,243 --> 00:12:49,464
He's been helping me out
with my legal things.

279
00:12:49,551 --> 00:12:51,379
I'd be lost without him.

280
00:12:51,466 --> 00:12:54,251
Tax liens and escrow,
nothing too complicated.

281
00:12:54,338 --> 00:12:56,036
[cell phone rings]

282
00:12:56,123 --> 00:12:57,037
Excuse me.

283
00:13:00,867 --> 00:13:02,259
Cho.

284
00:13:02,346 --> 00:13:05,175
Cho, did you ask
about the ghost?

285
00:13:05,262 --> 00:13:07,308
No, you heard Lisbon.

286
00:13:07,395 --> 00:13:08,918
Uh-h-h.

287
00:13:09,005 --> 00:13:10,572
I am talking
to the local historian.

288
00:13:10,659 --> 00:13:13,662
Apparently, Beckworth was
deeply involved in spiritualism.

289
00:13:13,749 --> 00:13:15,359
Liked to explore
the world beyond

290
00:13:15,446 --> 00:13:17,492
held seancesand all
that kind of malarkey.

291
00:13:17,579 --> 00:13:20,800
So ghosts are relevant, my
friend. This is very important.

292
00:13:22,932 --> 00:13:24,891
Cho, don't leave me hanging.

293
00:13:27,807 --> 00:13:29,721
Ma'am, do you know anything
about a ghost at the mansion?

294
00:13:29,809 --> 00:13:31,071
Of course.

295
00:13:31,158 --> 00:13:33,116
That's the spirit
of my grandfather

296
00:13:33,203 --> 00:13:34,639
Walter Beckworth.

297
00:13:34,726 --> 00:13:36,554
He used to visit me
when I was a child.

298
00:13:36,641 --> 00:13:39,819
- She says there's a ghost.
- Thank you.

299
00:13:39,906 --> 00:13:41,995
No surprise there.

300
00:13:42,082 --> 00:13:44,301
[sniffs]
Mmm. Clover honey?

301
00:13:44,388 --> 00:13:46,260
Yes, as a matter of fact it is.

302
00:13:46,347 --> 00:13:47,478
[Jane]
'Mmm.'

303
00:13:49,306 --> 00:13:52,005
- Jane?
- Mm. Sorry.

304
00:13:52,092 --> 00:13:55,182
Uh, Mr. Raimey says there's
also a hidden treasure there.

305
00:13:55,269 --> 00:13:57,488
Uh, rumored treasure,
purely rumored.

306
00:13:57,575 --> 00:14:00,840
Rumored treasure, some fortune
Beckworth hid in the house.

307
00:14:00,927 --> 00:14:03,190
[Jane on phone]
'Does Abner know
anything about that?'

308
00:14:03,277 --> 00:14:06,280
- Beckworth's treasure?
- Oh, yes.

309
00:14:06,367 --> 00:14:09,109
Everybody in the family
knew that there was a great

310
00:14:09,196 --> 00:14:12,721
mythic treasure. We gave up
looking for it years ago.

311
00:14:12,808 --> 00:14:16,899
It was a fraud. If it wasn't,
do you think I'd be living here?

312
00:14:16,986 --> 00:14:18,596
She says no treasure.

313
00:14:18,683 --> 00:14:20,947
Hmm. Got it. Thank you.

314
00:14:21,034 --> 00:14:22,035
Okay.

315
00:14:23,079 --> 00:14:23,993
No treasure.

316
00:14:25,473 --> 00:14:26,822
'Clover honey.'

317
00:14:29,825 --> 00:14:31,261
Ah.

318
00:14:31,348 --> 00:14:34,264
Well...ahem...thank you.

319
00:14:34,351 --> 00:14:36,397
[instrumental music]

320
00:14:36,484 --> 00:14:38,268
If there is anything
else I can do..

321
00:14:38,355 --> 00:14:39,487
Well, there is one more thing.

322
00:14:39,574 --> 00:14:41,619
H-how did Beckworth
make his money?

323
00:14:41,706 --> 00:14:44,884
Oh, he was a bootlegger,
very successful one.

324
00:14:44,971 --> 00:14:47,538
It's in, uh, that one,
the second from the bottom.

325
00:14:47,625 --> 00:14:49,627
Okay. Thank you.

326
00:14:49,714 --> 00:14:53,066
Uh, d-did you ever
cross paths with Foster?

327
00:14:53,153 --> 00:14:54,763
Once or twice.

328
00:14:54,850 --> 00:14:57,635
He seemed pleasant enough.
Never paid very much attention.

329
00:14:57,722 --> 00:14:59,811
You deceitful
old bag of bones.

330
00:14:59,899 --> 00:15:01,683
- Excuse me?
- You heard me.

331
00:15:06,035 --> 00:15:07,907
[Rigsby]
'Nothing on
the exterior of the house.'

332
00:15:07,994 --> 00:15:10,387
Let's check inside.

333
00:15:10,474 --> 00:15:11,606
- That's weird.
- What?

334
00:15:11,693 --> 00:15:13,521
Thought this was shut.

335
00:15:13,608 --> 00:15:14,783
[creaking]

336
00:15:20,789 --> 00:15:22,965
[rattling]

337
00:15:23,052 --> 00:15:26,751
- Did you hear that?
- Yeah. Someone's upstairs.

338
00:15:26,838 --> 00:15:28,188
I thought everybody left.

339
00:15:28,275 --> 00:15:31,147
[instrumental music]

340
00:15:33,410 --> 00:15:34,368
Hello?

341
00:15:43,725 --> 00:15:44,726
[thud]

342
00:15:46,858 --> 00:15:48,773
It came from in there.

343
00:15:56,216 --> 00:15:57,565
[sizzling]

344
00:15:57,652 --> 00:15:59,306
Old wiring, I guess.

345
00:15:59,393 --> 00:16:00,742
[man laughing]

346
00:16:00,829 --> 00:16:02,222
What was that?

347
00:16:02,309 --> 00:16:05,007
CBI. Come out.

348
00:16:05,094 --> 00:16:06,139
[door creaks]

349
00:16:07,053 --> 00:16:08,097
[door bangs shut]

350
00:16:09,925 --> 00:16:11,840
Police, whoever you are,
come out of the room

351
00:16:11,927 --> 00:16:13,189
'with your hands
above your head.'

352
00:16:14,582 --> 00:16:18,107
[male #2]
'Grace. Over here.'

353
00:16:20,631 --> 00:16:22,503
[instrumental music]

354
00:16:24,548 --> 00:16:25,506
Boo!

355
00:16:29,466 --> 00:16:31,425
[instrumental music]

356
00:16:31,512 --> 00:16:32,469
Jane?

357
00:16:33,383 --> 00:16:34,297
[door bangs shut]

358
00:16:40,564 --> 00:16:43,350
- Where'd he go?
- How'd he do that?

359
00:16:43,437 --> 00:16:45,961
It's easy. I'll show you.

360
00:16:47,658 --> 00:16:50,661
'Ah, you see?'

361
00:16:50,748 --> 00:16:52,707
It's all switches.

362
00:16:53,577 --> 00:16:56,450
[sizzling]

363
00:16:58,321 --> 00:16:59,714
There's a system
of pipes that carry

364
00:16:59,801 --> 00:17:00,715
sound throughout the house.

365
00:17:00,802 --> 00:17:02,195
[laughing]

366
00:17:04,110 --> 00:17:06,808
- Hello?
- Yeah. It's all fake.

367
00:17:06,895 --> 00:17:10,899
Because, my credulous Van Pelt,
spirits are smoke and mirrors

368
00:17:10,986 --> 00:17:12,509
and that's all that they are.

369
00:17:12,596 --> 00:17:13,728
And the ghost?
How did you do that?

370
00:17:13,815 --> 00:17:15,947
Ah, the piece de resistance.

371
00:17:16,035 --> 00:17:18,689
If you could stand in
the corner, my friend Rigsby

372
00:17:18,776 --> 00:17:21,823
and, Grace, if you could take
your place out by the doors.

373
00:17:21,910 --> 00:17:23,694
Pepper's ghost.

374
00:17:23,781 --> 00:17:27,785
It's an old conjurer's trick,
a simple optical illusion.

375
00:17:27,872 --> 00:17:30,484
All you need is a sheet of glass

376
00:17:30,571 --> 00:17:32,616
that slides out of the wall..

377
00:17:36,055 --> 00:17:37,926
It's a reflection?

378
00:17:38,013 --> 00:17:39,493
And lighting.

379
00:17:39,580 --> 00:17:42,409
[sizzling]

380
00:17:43,845 --> 00:17:45,020
[chuckles]

381
00:17:46,021 --> 00:17:48,415
[chuckling continues]

382
00:17:48,502 --> 00:17:50,069
And off.

383
00:17:53,028 --> 00:17:56,075
And Beckworth had all this built
just so he could hold seances?

384
00:17:56,162 --> 00:17:58,164
So he could amaze
and terrify his guests

385
00:17:58,251 --> 00:18:00,992
with his mastery of the occult,
and it's been used recently.

386
00:18:01,080 --> 00:18:02,994
Someone has
cleaned the dust off.

387
00:18:03,082 --> 00:18:04,866
So somebody was
trying to scare Foster.

388
00:18:04,953 --> 00:18:06,302
- Mm-hmm.
- Why?

389
00:18:06,389 --> 00:18:07,651
And how did they
get in the house?

390
00:18:07,738 --> 00:18:10,089
The alarm would have
been on the whole time.

391
00:18:10,176 --> 00:18:11,525
Come with me.

392
00:18:11,612 --> 00:18:13,831
Beckworth was a bootlegger
during prohibition

393
00:18:13,918 --> 00:18:15,572
which meant he had
to smuggle liquor

394
00:18:15,659 --> 00:18:17,226
into the house
and hide it someplace

395
00:18:17,313 --> 00:18:18,836
the authorities
couldn't find it.

396
00:18:18,923 --> 00:18:22,275
This house is riddled
with secret passages.

397
00:18:22,362 --> 00:18:24,320
Including ones
that lead outside?

398
00:18:28,194 --> 00:18:29,891
[creaking]

399
00:18:33,286 --> 00:18:34,200
Voila.

400
00:18:35,462 --> 00:18:36,506
Head down. Take a look.

401
00:18:36,593 --> 00:18:38,160
I'll check the outside walls.

402
00:18:38,247 --> 00:18:40,380
No doubt one of us will
find the secret passage.

403
00:18:42,643 --> 00:18:44,601
- After you.
- Oh, thank you.

404
00:18:51,826 --> 00:18:53,958
[indistinct singing]

405
00:18:55,046 --> 00:18:56,309
[bell ringing]

406
00:19:01,140 --> 00:19:03,707
- Jeb Haas?
- Who wants to know?

407
00:19:03,794 --> 00:19:08,364
- Agent Lisbon, CBI.
- Pretty little thing like you?

408
00:19:08,451 --> 00:19:12,238
I have some questions for you
regarding Alan Foster's murder.

409
00:19:12,325 --> 00:19:15,110
I got nothing to tell you.

410
00:19:15,197 --> 00:19:17,504
Looks like there were some
construction supplies stolen

411
00:19:17,591 --> 00:19:18,983
around the time of the murder.

412
00:19:19,070 --> 00:19:20,898
Deputy Fisher says
you're the local thief

413
00:19:20,985 --> 00:19:22,857
most likely to steal
that kind of stuff.

414
00:19:22,944 --> 00:19:25,816
Fisher sent you?
That son of a bitch.

415
00:19:25,903 --> 00:19:28,384
I don't know what
he told you but, uh..

416
00:19:28,471 --> 00:19:30,865
...I don't know anything
about a break-in or any murder.

417
00:19:30,952 --> 00:19:33,607
Then you won't mind coming
down to the station with me.

418
00:19:33,694 --> 00:19:35,348
What if I don't want to go?

419
00:19:35,435 --> 00:19:37,872
Sir, this doesn't
have to be difficult.

420
00:19:40,788 --> 00:19:43,704
[intense music]

421
00:19:49,057 --> 00:19:51,581
- You okay?
- Yeah, yeah. You?

422
00:20:01,112 --> 00:20:03,637
Hey, look at that.
Wine cellar.

423
00:20:03,724 --> 00:20:05,029
Wonder if any of
it's still good.

424
00:20:05,116 --> 00:20:06,596
I doubt it.

425
00:20:07,467 --> 00:20:09,251
[rustling]

426
00:20:09,338 --> 00:20:11,427
- What was that?
- Eh, probably rats.

427
00:20:11,514 --> 00:20:14,256
Oh, God, no. I hate rats.

428
00:20:14,343 --> 00:20:16,519
- Oh, it's just a ghost then.
- You don't believe in ghosts.

429
00:20:16,606 --> 00:20:18,434
Yeah, but you do.
Wait upstairs, if you like.

430
00:20:18,521 --> 00:20:21,742
- I'll take this one.
- Oh, thanks. That's okay.

431
00:20:27,138 --> 00:20:28,836
Same night that
Foster was killed

432
00:20:28,923 --> 00:20:31,230
you broke into his pool house
and stole a few thousand dollars

433
00:20:31,317 --> 00:20:33,101
worth of tools and equipment.

434
00:20:33,188 --> 00:20:34,929
We know because we found
some of the stolen equipment

435
00:20:35,016 --> 00:20:36,713
in your vehicle,
and we found this

436
00:20:36,800 --> 00:20:37,888
cigarette butt
in the pool house.

437
00:20:37,975 --> 00:20:39,977
It'll definitely
have your DNA on it.

438
00:20:42,197 --> 00:20:43,677
Everybody knows that
place is all wired up

439
00:20:43,764 --> 00:20:45,548
with some fancy alarm system.

440
00:20:45,635 --> 00:20:47,376
I didn't kill anybody.

441
00:20:47,463 --> 00:20:50,118
I never saw Foster, and I
never went into his house.

442
00:20:50,205 --> 00:20:52,773
- Nice if you could prove that.
- I can't.

443
00:20:52,860 --> 00:20:55,210
But I do have some
particular information

444
00:20:55,297 --> 00:20:57,734
on that evening...
information that you want.

445
00:20:59,649 --> 00:21:01,912
- Okay.
- Uh-uh.

446
00:21:01,999 --> 00:21:03,697
Not until I get
a deal on the theft

447
00:21:03,784 --> 00:21:06,003
and the breaking
and entering charges.

448
00:21:06,090 --> 00:21:09,268
- A good one too.
- You want a deal? Okay.

449
00:21:09,355 --> 00:21:11,052
And just to be clear,
is this a deal for the theft

450
00:21:11,139 --> 00:21:14,403
and B&E alone,
or the tax evasion as well?

451
00:21:14,490 --> 00:21:16,231
What?

452
00:21:16,318 --> 00:21:17,537
According to your IRS records

453
00:21:17,624 --> 00:21:19,930
you haven't filed
a tax return since 2002.

454
00:21:20,017 --> 00:21:21,976
Unless you haven't earned
a dime in income in seven years

455
00:21:22,063 --> 00:21:24,718
you're facing some pretty
serious tax evasion charges.

456
00:21:24,805 --> 00:21:27,460
Do you have any idea how much
prison time that carries?

457
00:21:27,547 --> 00:21:30,941
- Prison time?
- 'Serious time, massive fines.'

458
00:21:32,595 --> 00:21:34,510
I'm gonna let you
think about it.

459
00:21:36,860 --> 00:21:39,950
When I was loading stuff
out of the pool house..

460
00:21:40,037 --> 00:21:42,953
...I saw a car in
the woods, a black BMW.

461
00:21:43,040 --> 00:21:44,259
'There was somebody sitting in'

462
00:21:44,346 --> 00:21:47,262
the driver's seat...
Foster's wife.

463
00:21:47,349 --> 00:21:48,959
Lillian Foster?

464
00:21:49,046 --> 00:21:51,048
I've seen her in town.
She sat there the whole time.

465
00:21:51,135 --> 00:21:54,617
She never even noticed me.
She just stared at the house.

466
00:21:54,704 --> 00:21:57,403
Was she still
there when you left?

467
00:21:57,490 --> 00:21:58,404
Yes.

468
00:22:02,277 --> 00:22:04,975
So you'll do something,
I mean, about the tax thing?

469
00:22:06,368 --> 00:22:08,196
Don't worry.
I was lyin' about that.

470
00:22:19,686 --> 00:22:20,904
- What's wrong with it?
- I don't know.

471
00:22:20,991 --> 00:22:23,472
Maybe the batteries.

472
00:22:23,559 --> 00:22:24,604
Don'thitit.

473
00:22:24,691 --> 00:22:26,083
Fine. You fix it then.

474
00:22:26,170 --> 00:22:28,129
Let's just use mine.

475
00:22:29,957 --> 00:22:32,089
- Ah!
- What? What? What?

476
00:22:32,176 --> 00:22:33,482
Oh, I felt something
crawling up my leg.

477
00:22:33,569 --> 00:22:35,092
- Well, is it still there?
- No.

478
00:22:35,179 --> 00:22:36,746
It was probably just a ghost.

479
00:22:43,492 --> 00:22:45,015
- Grace?
- Yes?

480
00:22:47,104 --> 00:22:48,279
I..

481
00:22:50,325 --> 00:22:51,718
Never mind.

482
00:22:53,850 --> 00:22:56,636
- Maybe we should keep going.
- Yeah.

483
00:22:56,723 --> 00:22:58,507
Grace, I love you.

484
00:22:58,594 --> 00:23:00,509
I've loved you from
the first moment I met you.

485
00:23:00,596 --> 00:23:04,034
Screw the rules.
Screw the CBI. I need you.

486
00:23:05,993 --> 00:23:07,951
I, uh-uh...that's, um..

487
00:23:08,038 --> 00:23:10,301
Unless you stop me,
I'm gonna kiss you now.

488
00:23:11,781 --> 00:23:13,261
[rattling]

489
00:23:13,348 --> 00:23:14,480
Eureka!

490
00:23:16,133 --> 00:23:17,613
Found it!

491
00:23:23,489 --> 00:23:24,968
Good work.

492
00:23:25,055 --> 00:23:26,361
Yeah, we'll meet you
back at the office

493
00:23:26,448 --> 00:23:28,668
after we pick up
Lillian Foster.

494
00:23:28,755 --> 00:23:31,845
We're outside
her house now.

495
00:23:31,932 --> 00:23:33,890
Jane says he found another
entrance to the mansion.

496
00:23:33,977 --> 00:23:35,588
I knew there had
to be another way in.

497
00:23:35,675 --> 00:23:37,764
[Lillian]
'How dare you come here?
My husband just died.'

498
00:23:37,851 --> 00:23:39,330
Is that Victoria Abner
and her nephew?

499
00:23:39,418 --> 00:23:41,115
What are they doing here?

500
00:23:41,202 --> 00:23:42,595
Have you no shame?

501
00:23:42,682 --> 00:23:44,771
Mrs. Foster, if you could
just please calm down.

502
00:23:44,858 --> 00:23:46,294
You know what you are?
You're a vulture.

503
00:23:46,381 --> 00:23:47,861
You're a crazy,
obsessed old vulture!--

504
00:23:47,948 --> 00:23:50,385
That mansion is mine.
It belongs to myfamily.

505
00:23:50,472 --> 00:23:52,431
I will burn it down before
you step foot in that place!

506
00:23:52,518 --> 00:23:54,215
Lady, you need psychiatric help.

507
00:23:54,302 --> 00:23:55,564
- Oh, yeah?
- Hey-hey!

508
00:23:55,651 --> 00:23:58,611
[grunting]

509
00:23:58,698 --> 00:24:00,656
Hey! Break it up!

510
00:24:00,743 --> 00:24:02,658
Get her off of me now!

511
00:24:02,745 --> 00:24:04,704
What the hell is
the matter with you?

512
00:24:04,791 --> 00:24:06,445
You have no class!

513
00:24:06,532 --> 00:24:07,750
You're the one
that came to my house!

514
00:24:07,837 --> 00:24:10,797
[indistinct shouting]

515
00:24:10,884 --> 00:24:12,494
[Victoria]
'Oh, yeah, I'mthe crazy bitch?'

516
00:24:19,283 --> 00:24:21,851
How you feeling,
Mrs. Foster?

517
00:24:21,938 --> 00:24:24,680
Embarrassed.

518
00:24:24,767 --> 00:24:27,901
How'd you get into
the ruckus with Ms. Abner?

519
00:24:27,988 --> 00:24:31,382
She came by to ask if she could
rent the mansion back from me

520
00:24:31,470 --> 00:24:34,385
seeing as how I won't
want to be living there.

521
00:24:34,473 --> 00:24:35,691
Did she say why?

522
00:24:35,778 --> 00:24:38,607
I, uh, didn't wait
to hear the details.

523
00:24:38,694 --> 00:24:41,044
She had been plaguing us
all this time, and now this?

524
00:24:42,350 --> 00:24:45,527
[sighs]
I'm afraid I just...lost it.

525
00:24:48,574 --> 00:24:51,925
There is something else we
wanted to talk to you about.

526
00:24:52,012 --> 00:24:53,274
You weren't in San Francisco

527
00:24:53,361 --> 00:24:55,232
the night of your
husband's murder.

528
00:24:55,319 --> 00:24:57,670
You were at the mansion.

529
00:24:57,757 --> 00:24:59,323
You know about that?

530
00:24:59,410 --> 00:25:03,153
- Why'd you lie to us?
- I was ashamed.

531
00:25:03,240 --> 00:25:04,677
Of what?

532
00:25:04,764 --> 00:25:06,548
Of why I was there
in the first place.

533
00:25:06,635 --> 00:25:09,203
- And why was that?
- She was spying on her husband.

534
00:25:09,290 --> 00:25:11,988
She suspected him
of being unfaithful.

535
00:25:13,512 --> 00:25:14,991
Yes. How did you know that?

536
00:25:15,078 --> 00:25:17,516
Because he's been
unfaithful before?

537
00:25:21,258 --> 00:25:23,217
Yes.

538
00:25:23,304 --> 00:25:25,611
He had an affair
about a year ago.

539
00:25:25,698 --> 00:25:28,091
He was out of town,
I was caught up with my work

540
00:25:28,178 --> 00:25:31,921
and...it happened.

541
00:25:32,008 --> 00:25:33,619
That's very
understanding of you.

542
00:25:33,706 --> 00:25:36,360
Oh, I was furious, believe me.

543
00:25:36,447 --> 00:25:39,102
But we worked on it,
counseling and so forth

544
00:25:39,189 --> 00:25:41,540
and I thought I had moved on.

545
00:25:43,280 --> 00:25:46,109
But I hadn't.
I didn't trust him.

546
00:25:46,196 --> 00:25:48,764
So, when Alan told me he
would be spending this weekend

547
00:25:48,851 --> 00:25:51,419
at the mansion
and wouldn't be coming home..

548
00:25:53,160 --> 00:25:56,729
...I decided I had to go
there to catch him in the act.

549
00:25:56,816 --> 00:25:57,991
But no one showed up.

550
00:25:58,078 --> 00:26:00,080
No.

551
00:26:00,167 --> 00:26:03,387
I felt so awful that I
left without talking to him.

552
00:26:03,474 --> 00:26:06,565
- What time did you leave?
- Around ten.

553
00:26:08,175 --> 00:26:10,133
Is there any way
to prove that?

554
00:26:10,220 --> 00:26:13,789
Um, I bought gas
on the way home.

555
00:26:13,876 --> 00:26:16,357
The credit card company will
have a record of that, right?

556
00:26:16,444 --> 00:26:18,359
Yes, we'll verify that.

557
00:26:20,491 --> 00:26:23,233
I can't help thinking
that if I'd stayed there

558
00:26:23,320 --> 00:26:24,844
if I'd just gone
and talked to Alan

559
00:26:24,931 --> 00:26:27,150
he would still be alive.

560
00:26:27,237 --> 00:26:29,588
Mmm, most likely
you'd both be dead.

561
00:26:31,241 --> 00:26:34,157
With the way that I feel,
I might prefer that.

562
00:26:34,244 --> 00:26:36,899
That feeling will pass.

563
00:26:36,986 --> 00:26:39,162
What do you know about that?

564
00:26:39,249 --> 00:26:40,729
Enough.

565
00:26:46,648 --> 00:26:47,823
- How you doing, Drew?
- Fine.

566
00:26:47,910 --> 00:26:49,695
Thank you. Thanks.

567
00:26:49,782 --> 00:26:51,392
Now your aunt's gonna be
in with us a little longer.

568
00:26:51,479 --> 00:26:53,307
This fight's gonna take
some sorting out, okay?

569
00:26:53,394 --> 00:26:55,048
[sighs]
I tried to stop it
from escalating.

570
00:26:55,135 --> 00:26:56,702
That's why I was there
in the first place

571
00:26:56,789 --> 00:26:59,661
but the hostility just
kind of caught me off guard.

572
00:26:59,748 --> 00:27:01,707
It'll be up to your aunt
whether or not to press charges

573
00:27:01,794 --> 00:27:03,447
but you're a lawyer.
You know the deal.

574
00:27:03,534 --> 00:27:05,232
Well, I'm not
exactly a lawyer.

575
00:27:05,319 --> 00:27:08,452
My aunt likes to exaggerate.
I'm a paralegal.

576
00:27:08,539 --> 00:27:10,150
Cho, uh, I'm sorry.
You got a second?

577
00:27:10,237 --> 00:27:12,543
Sure. We're still gonna
need a statement from you.

578
00:27:12,631 --> 00:27:14,023
Excuse me for a second.

579
00:27:15,677 --> 00:27:18,201
- What's up?
- I told Grace I love her.

580
00:27:18,288 --> 00:27:20,943
Wow. Okay. Well done.
Then what happened?

581
00:27:21,030 --> 00:27:22,205
Oh, nothing,
Jane interrupted

582
00:27:22,292 --> 00:27:23,859
and she hasn't
said a word since.

583
00:27:23,946 --> 00:27:26,732
- So tell her again.
- Right, okay.

584
00:27:26,819 --> 00:27:28,690
Tell her again.
Yeah, alright.

585
00:27:33,390 --> 00:27:36,480
Your aura is
fascinating, Mr. Jane.

586
00:27:36,567 --> 00:27:38,134
So many hues.

587
00:27:38,221 --> 00:27:40,659
Thank you.

588
00:27:40,746 --> 00:27:44,184
The mansion's uninhabitable.
Why would you want it back?

589
00:27:44,271 --> 00:27:46,099
Sentimental reasons.

590
00:27:46,186 --> 00:27:48,188
Mmm, no.

591
00:27:48,275 --> 00:27:50,059
No, that's not right.

592
00:27:50,146 --> 00:27:52,627
You're closed, furtive.
You're hiding something.

593
00:27:52,714 --> 00:27:54,324
There's something in
that house, isn't there?

594
00:27:54,411 --> 00:27:57,588
Something you don't want
anyone else to find..

595
00:27:57,676 --> 00:27:59,852
...evidence of
your guilt, perhaps.

596
00:27:59,939 --> 00:28:01,418
That's absurd.

597
00:28:01,505 --> 00:28:03,594
No, yeah, you're right.
That is absurd.

598
00:28:03,682 --> 00:28:06,423
It's something
else entirely.

599
00:28:06,510 --> 00:28:09,296
Ah...the treasure.

600
00:28:09,383 --> 00:28:10,471
[chuckles]

601
00:28:10,558 --> 00:28:11,559
What treasure?

602
00:28:11,646 --> 00:28:12,691
Walter Beckworth's treasure

603
00:28:12,778 --> 00:28:15,519
he hid in the mansion.

604
00:28:15,606 --> 00:28:16,738
I wanted my house back

605
00:28:16,825 --> 00:28:17,783
because I didn't like

606
00:28:17,870 --> 00:28:19,045
what Foster planned for it.

607
00:28:19,132 --> 00:28:21,003
He was going to destroy it.

608
00:28:21,090 --> 00:28:23,049
[Lisbon]
'Foster had only
been there a few weeks.'

609
00:28:23,136 --> 00:28:25,834
How could you have known what
he was gonna do before he did?

610
00:28:25,921 --> 00:28:29,142
In conversation, he told me.

611
00:28:29,229 --> 00:28:32,493
Ma'am, we find out you're lying,
you become suspect number one.

612
00:28:36,715 --> 00:28:38,107
Alright.

613
00:28:40,719 --> 00:28:42,677
How are you with riddles?

614
00:28:45,201 --> 00:28:46,202
[chuckles]

615
00:28:46,289 --> 00:28:48,639
I found the clue
a few weeks ago

616
00:28:48,727 --> 00:28:50,380
when I bought some
of Beckworth's papers

617
00:28:50,467 --> 00:28:51,947
at an estate sale.

618
00:28:52,034 --> 00:28:54,210
It was in a letter
to one of his cousins

619
00:28:54,297 --> 00:28:55,908
just before his death.

620
00:28:55,995 --> 00:28:59,085
Beckworth boasts that he's
hidden his great treasure

621
00:28:59,172 --> 00:29:00,913
where no one
can find it

622
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:03,002
and then he
offers this clue

623
00:29:03,089 --> 00:29:05,569
"If fortune is
what you seek sincere

624
00:29:05,656 --> 00:29:07,615
the only place
to look is here."

625
00:29:08,877 --> 00:29:09,922
That's it?
That's the riddle?

626
00:29:10,009 --> 00:29:11,837
- Yes.
- Interesting.

627
00:29:11,924 --> 00:29:13,839
Of course, I told
Mrs. Abner right away

628
00:29:13,926 --> 00:29:16,015
but the house had already
been sold to Foster.

629
00:29:16,102 --> 00:29:17,799
Why didn't you
mention this before?

630
00:29:17,886 --> 00:29:20,541
Well, it-it...
it didn't seem relevant.

631
00:29:20,628 --> 00:29:23,326
It's motive. It's very relevant.

632
00:29:23,413 --> 00:29:25,894
Mr. Raimey and Ms. Abner
had agreed to keep quiet

633
00:29:25,981 --> 00:29:29,158
to avoid having to share the
loot with anyone else, correct?

634
00:29:29,245 --> 00:29:32,248
Yes, but that doesn't mean
either of us killed Foster.

635
00:29:32,335 --> 00:29:33,380
Oh, absolutely right.

636
00:29:33,467 --> 00:29:34,990
Whoever killed
Foster was simply

637
00:29:35,077 --> 00:29:38,167
trying to scare
him from the mansion.

638
00:29:38,254 --> 00:29:40,169
It could have been anyone
that wanted the treasure

639
00:29:40,256 --> 00:29:42,041
even you, Deputy Fisher.

640
00:29:42,128 --> 00:29:43,520
That's right. I killed him.

641
00:29:43,607 --> 00:29:45,392
Did you? You denied it earlier.

642
00:29:45,479 --> 00:29:46,785
Stop doing that.

643
00:29:46,872 --> 00:29:48,743
I need to see this clue.
Where's the letter?

644
00:29:56,185 --> 00:29:57,491
Thank you.

645
00:29:59,406 --> 00:30:00,929
[sniffs]

646
00:30:01,016 --> 00:30:02,757
- What?
- Smells good.

647
00:30:02,844 --> 00:30:06,543
"If fortune is
what you seek sincere

648
00:30:06,630 --> 00:30:12,027
the only place
to look is... hear."

649
00:30:12,114 --> 00:30:14,290
"Hear."

650
00:30:14,377 --> 00:30:17,076
It's spelled H-E-A-R.

651
00:30:17,163 --> 00:30:20,122
Well, I assumed
it was a mistake.

652
00:30:20,209 --> 00:30:22,124
Hmm. There are no mistakes.

653
00:30:22,211 --> 00:30:23,386
Let's go.

654
00:30:24,910 --> 00:30:27,738
[instrumental music]

655
00:30:28,827 --> 00:30:29,958
[door closes]

656
00:30:31,786 --> 00:30:34,571
[instrumental music]

657
00:30:39,620 --> 00:30:41,752
[indistinct chattering]

658
00:30:41,840 --> 00:30:46,148
[clears throat]
As you know, Walter Beckworth
left a coded message

659
00:30:46,235 --> 00:30:49,499
describing the location
of his treasure.

660
00:30:49,586 --> 00:30:51,414
I brought you all here
to help me find it.

661
00:30:51,501 --> 00:30:55,114
The word "here" is
misspelled in the riddle.

662
00:30:56,506 --> 00:30:58,508
I don't believe
that's a mistake.

663
00:30:58,595 --> 00:31:00,162
I think..

664
00:31:00,249 --> 00:31:01,772
...that the clue itself

665
00:31:01,860 --> 00:31:03,470
lies in that word

666
00:31:03,557 --> 00:31:07,169
hear, H-E-A-R.

667
00:31:08,867 --> 00:31:10,999
What comes to mind
when I say the word hear?

668
00:31:13,959 --> 00:31:15,047
Noise.

669
00:31:17,005 --> 00:31:19,181
- Sounds.
- Ears.

670
00:31:19,268 --> 00:31:21,967
Very good. What else?

671
00:31:22,054 --> 00:31:23,359
Music.

672
00:31:23,446 --> 00:31:24,447
Musical instruments.

673
00:31:24,534 --> 00:31:25,709
Definite possibility.

674
00:31:30,149 --> 00:31:31,324
Cho?

675
00:31:31,411 --> 00:31:34,153
[instrumental music]

676
00:31:38,548 --> 00:31:40,028
Unveil it.

677
00:31:47,993 --> 00:31:49,081
[creaks]

678
00:31:52,911 --> 00:31:54,042
[piano music]

679
00:32:03,269 --> 00:32:05,532
[tapping]

680
00:32:06,489 --> 00:32:09,101
[tapping continues]

681
00:32:09,840 --> 00:32:13,322
Mmm. Aha.

682
00:32:13,409 --> 00:32:14,628
What is it?

683
00:32:14,715 --> 00:32:15,890
Yeah, what is it?

684
00:32:31,123 --> 00:32:32,472
Beckworth's fortune.

685
00:32:42,743 --> 00:32:44,484
[exhales deeply]

686
00:32:44,571 --> 00:32:46,399
[Raimey]
'There's nothing in it.'

687
00:32:46,486 --> 00:32:47,835
[Drew]
'It's empty?'

688
00:32:47,922 --> 00:32:48,967
[Jane]
Wait.

689
00:32:57,888 --> 00:32:58,933
[Victoria]
'What is that?'

690
00:32:59,020 --> 00:33:00,369
It's another riddle.

691
00:33:00,456 --> 00:33:02,154
What does it say?

692
00:33:02,241 --> 00:33:05,070
"To find fortune and riches,
one doesn't need help.

693
00:33:05,157 --> 00:33:08,247
All one must do
is look to oneself."

694
00:33:13,556 --> 00:33:15,167
What does it mean?

695
00:33:15,254 --> 00:33:17,386
I don't know,
but it's spelled correctly.

696
00:33:17,473 --> 00:33:19,606
It means we're
wasting our time.

697
00:33:19,693 --> 00:33:21,086
It doesn't even make any sense.

698
00:33:21,173 --> 00:33:22,826
Let's not rush to judgment.

699
00:33:22,913 --> 00:33:25,525
Beckworth probably put it
there as a practical joke.

700
00:33:25,612 --> 00:33:26,874
Pretty lame joke.

701
00:33:26,961 --> 00:33:28,223
But the fortune
could still be here.

702
00:33:28,310 --> 00:33:29,746
I just need time to find it.

703
00:33:29,833 --> 00:33:31,096
We're not gonna
waste any more time.

704
00:33:31,183 --> 00:33:33,881
I'm shutting this down.
Everybody out.

705
00:33:33,968 --> 00:33:35,665
'Cho, secure the place
and set the alarm.'

706
00:33:35,752 --> 00:33:38,277
'Have Rigsby and Van Pelt
set up on the mansion tonight.'

707
00:33:38,364 --> 00:33:39,843
'We'll take
the shift after them.'

708
00:33:39,930 --> 00:33:41,019
Nobody gets in this place

709
00:33:41,106 --> 00:33:42,585
especially Jane. Understood?

710
00:33:42,672 --> 00:33:44,544
- Got it. Everybody out.
- 'L-Lisbon.'

711
00:33:44,631 --> 00:33:46,589
Lisbon. I, you, I-I,
this, Lisbon, this--

712
00:33:46,676 --> 00:33:48,722
Bite me.
We're done for the night.

713
00:33:56,860 --> 00:33:58,514
"Bite me?"

714
00:33:58,601 --> 00:34:01,126
- It's too much?
- Uh, a smidge.

715
00:34:04,564 --> 00:34:06,870
Worked. It's fine.

716
00:34:06,957 --> 00:34:09,134
Just a little over-the-top.

717
00:34:09,221 --> 00:34:12,093
[crickets chirping]

718
00:34:30,764 --> 00:34:32,026
- So, uh, Grace..
- Yes?

719
00:34:32,113 --> 00:34:34,159
...I was wondering
if we could..

720
00:34:34,246 --> 00:34:37,292
- How do you wanna handle this?
- Handle what?

721
00:34:37,379 --> 00:34:38,946
The stakeout.

722
00:34:39,033 --> 00:34:41,122
Oh. Um, right.

723
00:34:42,732 --> 00:34:44,952
Listen, about
the other thing..

724
00:34:45,039 --> 00:34:46,649
...it's, I, uh..

725
00:34:49,348 --> 00:34:51,611
Maybe it's not
the right time.

726
00:34:51,698 --> 00:34:53,221
Yeah. Yeah.

727
00:34:53,308 --> 00:34:56,398
Um...we could, uh, walk.

728
00:34:57,573 --> 00:35:00,402
Patrol the grounds.

729
00:35:00,489 --> 00:35:02,535
Or maybe one of us
could stay here

730
00:35:02,622 --> 00:35:05,233
while the other one
patrols alternate?

731
00:35:05,320 --> 00:35:07,844
Yep, that's better.
I'll, uh, I'll go first.

732
00:35:07,931 --> 00:35:10,064
Oh, that's okay.
I can do it.

733
00:35:10,151 --> 00:35:12,022
[Rigsby]
I'm easy.

734
00:35:12,110 --> 00:35:13,633
[Grace]
'Well, if you wanna go
first, that's fine also.'

735
00:35:13,720 --> 00:35:16,244
[Rigsby]
No, no. No.

736
00:35:23,860 --> 00:35:26,776
[intense music]

737
00:35:35,307 --> 00:35:36,830
[creaking]

738
00:35:49,103 --> 00:35:50,452
"Look to oneself."

739
00:35:53,238 --> 00:35:56,066
[music continues]

740
00:36:04,640 --> 00:36:07,295
[shattering]

741
00:36:08,209 --> 00:36:09,297
No.

742
00:36:11,560 --> 00:36:14,215
There's nothin' there,
Drew. Never was.

743
00:36:15,608 --> 00:36:17,392
You planted the second clue?

744
00:36:17,479 --> 00:36:19,525
Aha. Yes.

745
00:36:21,483 --> 00:36:22,745
I didn't kill Foster.

746
00:36:22,832 --> 00:36:24,617
Don't be ridiculous.
Of course, you did.

747
00:36:24,704 --> 00:36:26,184
Only the killer knew
how to get in here

748
00:36:26,271 --> 00:36:27,837
viathe secret passageway.

749
00:36:27,924 --> 00:36:30,275
You proved your guilt
just by walking in the room.

750
00:36:30,362 --> 00:36:31,450
[sighs]

751
00:36:31,537 --> 00:36:33,408
Was it all just
for the money?

752
00:36:38,631 --> 00:36:40,589
"Justfor the money?"

753
00:36:42,461 --> 00:36:45,551
I spent years listening
to my family go on

754
00:36:45,638 --> 00:36:48,118
about the Beckworths
and everything that they had.

755
00:36:49,772 --> 00:36:51,165
'I didn't get any of it.'

756
00:36:52,166 --> 00:36:55,125
Not one thing.

757
00:36:55,213 --> 00:36:57,606
And then my aunt told me
about the clue and the treasure

758
00:36:57,693 --> 00:37:01,523
and I thought...I could
get that for myself.

759
00:37:01,610 --> 00:37:05,919
I could have what I've
deserved all my life.

760
00:37:06,006 --> 00:37:08,617
All I had to do was get
Foster out of the house.

761
00:37:08,704 --> 00:37:11,838
So you haunted him.

762
00:37:11,925 --> 00:37:14,580
My cousin told me
about the, uh, equipment

763
00:37:14,667 --> 00:37:17,322
Beckworth had built in.

764
00:37:17,409 --> 00:37:18,932
I just wanted to scare him.

765
00:37:20,890 --> 00:37:22,892
[Drew]
But Foster
wouldn't take a hint.

766
00:37:25,460 --> 00:37:27,332
So I had to do something.

767
00:37:31,684 --> 00:37:34,034
[growly]
You.

768
00:37:44,262 --> 00:37:46,264
Hey! Hey!

769
00:37:46,351 --> 00:37:49,310
Hey, hey, stop!
Hey, stop!

770
00:37:51,573 --> 00:37:53,358
I didn't mean to kill him.

771
00:37:53,445 --> 00:37:56,230
[intense music]

772
00:38:00,234 --> 00:38:02,889
Oh, I didn't know what to do.

773
00:38:02,976 --> 00:38:06,936
And then, uh, I remembered
the stories about Beckworth

774
00:38:07,023 --> 00:38:11,158
how he was killed, how his
ghost still haunts this place.

775
00:38:11,245 --> 00:38:12,681
So I threw him out the window

776
00:38:12,768 --> 00:38:14,422
the same way
Beckworth was killed.

777
00:38:16,381 --> 00:38:20,602
It wasn't Foster's house.
It belonged to myfamily.

778
00:38:20,689 --> 00:38:23,301
Put your hands
behind your back.Now.

779
00:38:29,785 --> 00:38:31,526
When you first opened
the box under the piano

780
00:38:31,613 --> 00:38:33,572
was there anything in it?

781
00:38:33,659 --> 00:38:35,791
Mmm..

782
00:38:35,878 --> 00:38:37,053
...this.

783
00:38:38,881 --> 00:38:41,014
You killed Foster
for an old key.

784
00:38:43,146 --> 00:38:44,974
I wonder what it opens.

785
00:38:45,061 --> 00:38:47,499
- Oh, I know what it opens.
- What?

786
00:38:50,937 --> 00:38:54,114
He's just messing with you.
He's mean like that.

787
00:38:54,201 --> 00:38:55,507
'Come on.'

788
00:39:03,993 --> 00:39:05,560
Mr. Jane.

789
00:39:05,647 --> 00:39:07,693
I was just being briefed
by one of your agents.

790
00:39:07,780 --> 00:39:09,259
Well, I hope they
conducted themselves

791
00:39:09,347 --> 00:39:10,913
in a professional manner.

792
00:39:12,437 --> 00:39:15,048
Thank you...for everything.

793
00:39:15,135 --> 00:39:18,181
Well, my pleasure.

794
00:39:18,268 --> 00:39:20,183
Do you mind a question?

795
00:39:21,794 --> 00:39:23,709
Uh...no.

796
00:39:26,189 --> 00:39:28,714
I asked about you.

797
00:39:28,801 --> 00:39:32,979
What happened to your
family, I'm very sorry.

798
00:39:33,066 --> 00:39:36,461
And I thought...maybe
it means you understand

799
00:39:36,548 --> 00:39:39,246
how it feels
to lose somebody.

800
00:39:39,333 --> 00:39:41,030
Was that your question?

801
00:39:43,685 --> 00:39:45,600
Do you think they
know how we feel?

802
00:39:47,297 --> 00:39:50,083
The dead, I mean..

803
00:39:50,170 --> 00:39:52,607
...how sorry we are..

804
00:39:52,694 --> 00:39:54,653
...how we wish things
could be different?

805
00:39:56,742 --> 00:39:59,135
[crying]
Do you think
they understand?

806
00:39:59,222 --> 00:40:02,095
[instrumental music]

807
00:40:05,359 --> 00:40:06,447
No.

808
00:40:10,799 --> 00:40:11,887
Sorry.

809
00:40:12,975 --> 00:40:13,889
[sniffles]

810
00:40:23,072 --> 00:40:24,291
Case closed pizza?

811
00:40:24,378 --> 00:40:25,771
Case closed pizza.

812
00:40:25,858 --> 00:40:27,729
Rigsby eats all the sausage
again, I'll be pissed.

813
00:40:29,470 --> 00:40:31,864
- Are you okay?
- Right as rain.

814
00:40:31,951 --> 00:40:34,083
Oh, I have the perfect
thing to go with this.

815
00:40:44,398 --> 00:40:45,834
- Wine?
- Yeah.

816
00:40:45,921 --> 00:40:47,880
Just a little
something special.

817
00:40:53,102 --> 00:40:55,975
Excuse me.
I need a glass. Sorry.

818
00:40:56,062 --> 00:40:57,193
Sure.

819
00:41:04,766 --> 00:41:07,465
[instrumental music]

820
00:41:22,175 --> 00:41:24,960
[music continues]

821
00:41:34,579 --> 00:41:36,015
I'm gonna leave
in a few minutes.

822
00:41:36,102 --> 00:41:37,582
Wait five,
then meet me at my car.

823
00:41:37,669 --> 00:41:39,540
- Okay.
- Okay.

824
00:41:41,803 --> 00:41:44,502
[music continues]

825
00:41:48,418 --> 00:41:49,768
Just a little.

826
00:41:56,252 --> 00:41:59,299
- Please.
- Thanks.

827
00:41:59,386 --> 00:42:01,780
- Mr. Rigsby.
- Cheers.

828
00:42:04,870 --> 00:42:05,827
Case closed.

829
00:42:05,914 --> 00:42:07,525
[all]
Case closed.

830
00:42:09,135 --> 00:42:11,093
- Mmm. It's nice.
- Glad you like it.

831
00:42:11,180 --> 00:42:13,182
So what was the key to, Jane?
The one you found in the box.

832
00:42:13,269 --> 00:42:16,490
- The key? Beckworth's fortune.
- Give it up.

833
00:42:16,577 --> 00:42:18,884
Well, just because the fortune
wasn't gold or cash

834
00:42:18,971 --> 00:42:21,713
doesn't mean it doesn't exist.

835
00:42:21,800 --> 00:42:24,237
- So what was it?
- Beckworth was a collector.

836
00:42:24,324 --> 00:42:26,805
He collected
hundreds of one thing

837
00:42:26,892 --> 00:42:29,024
some of the finest,
rarest examples in the world

838
00:42:29,111 --> 00:42:30,852
worth tens
of millions of dollars.

839
00:42:30,939 --> 00:42:32,593
- So what was it?
- Mmm.

840
00:42:32,680 --> 00:42:34,290
- 'What?'
- Smells good.

841
00:42:34,377 --> 00:42:37,163
[instrumental music]

842
00:42:44,866 --> 00:42:45,824
Cheers.

843
00:42:45,911 --> 00:42:47,652
[instrumental music]

844
00:42:54,397 --> 00:42:56,574
[theme music]

